statistics Plugin for DokuWiki

Install from URL:

  * https://github.com/tracyhatemice/dokuwiki-plugin-statistics/zipball/master


All documentation for this plugin can be found at
http://www.dokuwiki.org/plugin:statistics

If you install this plugin manually, make sure it is installed in
lib/plugins/statistics/ - if the folder is called different it
will not work!

Please refer to http://www.dokuwiki.org/plugins for additional info
on how to install plugins in DokuWiki.

Audit log
---------

Events that other plugins write through DokuWiki's native logger
(\dokuwiki\Logger::getInstance('<facility>')->log(...)) can be captured into
the statistics database and browsed under Admin > Statistics > (Audit):
a dashboard with counters, an events-per-facility trend and quick tables,
a filterable event log (facility, user, action, ip, free text), summaries
by action, by user and by IP with trend graphs that link into the log,
and a user-by-action matrix.

  * audit_facilities  comma separated facilities to capture, e.g.
                      facility1,facility2
                      Empty disables auditing. Facilities in the core
                      "dontlog" setting are never captured.
  * audit_include     comma separated facility:action patterns (shell
                      wildcards, action optional); when set, only matching
                      events are stored, e.g. logged:edit, logged:save
  * audit_exclude     patterns whose events are not stored, e.g.
                      logged:show, logged:media, *:debug_*
                      Exclusions win. Excluded events still reach the files.
  * audit_keepfiles   keep writing the file logs as well (default on)
  * audit_retention   days to keep audit rows, 0 = forever
  * audit_subject_keys  comma separated detail keys tried, in order, for
                      the subject column (default id,page). Add keys your
                      plugins emit, e.g. id,page

Each event is stored with facility, user, ip, action, subject, message and
the original details. All known sources write "<user> <action> ..." as the
message and a compact JSON object as details; user/ip/action come from
that JSON when present, otherwise from the request. The subject is the
first of the configured subject keys found in the details.

Other plugins can adjust or drop a row before it is stored by handling the
PLUGIN_STATISTICS_AUDIT_RECORD event (BEFORE, preventable). The event data
is the row: dt, facility, user, ip, action, subject, message, details.

The audit pages are visible to superusers only. The statistics privacy
settings anonips and nousers do not apply to audit rows.

